PUSH VS PULL
=============

Push vs. pull for HRS
	Push (event/webhook/message)
		Event sources publish to a broker or invoke an endpoint; workers consume immediately, giving near‑real‑time start and horizontal fan‑out via consumer groups/partitions. This minimizes detection latency and scales by adding consumers.
	Ideal for alert resolution and BCP triggers where every second matters; polling intervals otherwise add avoidable delay.

Pull (polling/long‑polling)
	Workers regularly check for work; simple to implement but introduces latency tied to poll interval and can waste calls when no work exists.
	Useful when producers can’t send outbound calls (firewalls), when dedup/batching is desired, or for high‑variance, non‑critical checks.

When to use pull
	Network constraints: on‑prem systems allow outbound-only connections from cloud, blocking webhooks.
	Back‑pressure: throttle concurrency by limiting worker count and per‑action concurrency to protect fragile systems.
	Batching: collect N items or T seconds of items before acting (e.g., group remediation).
	Untrusted producers: avoid exposing public endpoints; workers fetch from a private, authenticated queue/topic.




Recommendation
	Choose push for most production alert remediation and BCP triggers to minimize detection-to-action time and enable elastic fan‑out; reserve pull where endpoints cannot accept inbound calls, where batching is valuable, or where tight back‑pressure is needed.

Why push first
	Lower latency: actions start as soon as an event is produced, avoiding poll intervals or sensor delays.
	Elastic throughput: scale consumers horizontally on queue/topic partitions; the broker buffers bursts without manual tuning.
	Clear causality: each event directly drives an action, simplifying tracing, idempotency keys, and auditing.

Where pull shines
	Network constraints: on‑prem systems behind firewalls that only allow outbound connections from the cloud side.
	Back‑pressure safety: workers meter their own intake (poll rate, max in‑flight) to protect fragile downstreams.
	Batching and periodicity: combine many small signals into one action, or perform scheduled condition checks.

Practical HRS pattern
	Use an event/broker layer for push (alerts, test schedules) and action workers that auto‑scale on backlog; attach DLQs, retries with jitter, and per‑tenant quotas.
	Add a pull connector tier for on‑prem integrations: long‑polling, adaptive intervals, circuit breakers, and idempotent writes; keep tasks small with payloads stored by reference.

Tenant and governance fit
	Enforce tenant isolation at the broker (partitions/queues per tenant or strong keys) and at workers (scoped credentials), regardless of push or pull.
	Expose mode per action type in Avertix: “push” by default; “pull” for constrained connectors, with policy‑controlled poll windows, concurrency caps, and audit of every fetch/execute/ack.

Decision checklist
	Target start latency under a few seconds → push.
	Endpoint cannot receive inbound traffic / requires batching → pull.
	Unpredictable surges with strict SLOs → push with autoscaling, broker buffering.
	Highly sensitive or rate‑limited systems → pull with tight concurrency and adaptive backoff.



